# ============================================================================
# Outlaw OS - live Boot Manager package set
# Installed packages for the actual on-disk OS are pacstrapped by outlaw-install
# (it reuses this same list). Everything here is from the official Arch repos
# (core/extra/multilib) so the ISO builds without AUR.
#
# THE DEFAULT INSTALL IS MINIMAL — the OS only pre-bundles what's needed to
# (1) boot securely, (2) drive a stripped-down Electron shell, (3) run Steam,
# (4) edit Godot projects with Outlaw CodeMaker, and (5) talk to LM Studio.
# Everything else (Blender, GIMP, VS Code, Lutris, Wine, security toolkit,
# alternative browsers, etc.) is available on demand via the Apps panel in
# the shell, which calls `pkexec pacman -S <pkg>` for the user.
# ============================================================================

# --- Base system ---
base
linux
linux-firmware
intel-ucode
amd-ucode
mkinitcpio
sudo
polkit
networkmanager
curl
wget
git
nano
# pacman-contrib provides `checkupdates` — the System Package Updates panel uses
# it to count available updates safely (without touching the live database).
pacman-contrib
# zram-generator powers the optional compressed-RAM swap that the "Tune This
# PC" panel (outlaw-tune) enables on low-memory machines. Tiny; no effect
# unless outlaw-tune writes a zram config.
zram-generator

# --- Bootloader / install tooling ---
# syslinux is required by the bios.syslinux.* bootmodes declared in
# profiledef.sh — without it mkarchiso fails ISO validation before build.
# mkinitcpio-archiso provides the live-init hooks the boot image needs;
# missing it produces a USB stick that boots to a kernel panic.
syslinux
mkinitcpio-archiso
grub
efibootmgr
os-prober
dosfstools
mtools
arch-install-scripts
gptfdisk
parted
# memtest86+ and edk2-shell are referenced by the upstream releng GRUB menu
# we inherit. They're not strictly required to boot, but missing them
# produces noisy warnings during validation and the corresponding boot-menu
# entries silently disappear. Cheap to include.
memtest86+
memtest86+-efi
edk2-shell
# gparted is INSTALLED IN THE LIVE ISO ONLY (not in the on-disk install set in
# outlaw-install's PKGS array) so first-time users can resize Windows / other
# OS partitions from the live environment if they need to make space for
# Outlaw, without bloating the post-install footprint.
gparted
# ntfs-3g provides ntfsresize, which the installer's "install alongside
# Windows" path uses to shrink NTFS partitions automatically (probed
# read-only first; refuses dirty volumes). gparted only lists it as an
# optional dependency, so it must be named explicitly here.
ntfs-3g

# --- Security hardening ---
apparmor
firejail
ufw

# --- Desktop / shell runtime ---
xorg-server
xorg-xinit
# Xorg video drivers. The primary path is the kernel's "modesetting" driver
# over whatever KMS driver the GPU exposes — vmwgfx (VirtualBox VMSVGA),
# vboxvideo (VirtualBox VBoxVGA), or a real GPU's driver. These two extras are
# automatic fallbacks Xorg tries if modesetting can't open a screen:
#   * xf86-video-vesa  — works via the real-mode VESA BIOS (BIOS boot).
#   * xf86-video-fbdev — renders to a kernel framebuffer (/dev/fb0), e.g. efifb.
# (xf86-video-vmware was dropped from the Arch repos; vmwgfx in-kernel covers
# VMSVGA.) We do NOT force a specific driver or use `nomodeset` — letting Xorg
# auto-select is what works across BIOS, UEFI, VMs, and real hardware.
xf86-video-vesa
xf86-video-fbdev
picom
feh
rofi
thunar
xfce4-terminal
electron
# fuse2: lets AppImages (e.g. the LM Studio download) mount and run. Without it
# AppImages won't launch at all.
fuse2
# openbox: a tiny window manager so launched app windows get real title bars
# (minimize / maximize / close) + drag/resize. tint2: a taskbar showing every
# window, click to restore minimized ones. The Outlaw shell stays the frameless
# full-screen backdrop; apps float above it. Both are guarded in .xinitrc — if
# they're absent the session falls back to the old no-WM kiosk.
openbox
tint2

# --- Audio (PipeWire) ---
# WITHOUT THIS THE SYSTEM HAS NO SOUND. pipewire-pulse / pipewire-alsa give
# Pulse/ALSA app compatibility (games, browsers, Godot); wireplumber is the
# session manager; alsa-utils provides amixer/alsactl and backs the desktop's
# topbar volume control. Socket-activated by the user's systemd instance on
# login (pam_systemd) — no manual start needed.
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
pipewire-alsa
wireplumber
alsa-utils

ttf-jetbrains-mono
noto-fonts

# --- Local AI ---
# LM Studio (https://lmstudio.ai) is not in Arch repos — it's an AppImage the
# user downloads at first launch via /usr/local/bin/outlaw-lm-studio. We only
# pre-install a launcher (the shell calls it for the AI assistant).

# --- GPU stack for the live shell ---
# We only ship mesa in the live ISO — enough to run the Electron shell, the
# installer, GParted, and the welcome card. The FULL gaming stack (Steam +
# gamescope + gamemode + Vulkan + lib32-*) is installed onto the user's disk
# by outlaw-install — see the PKGS array in airootfs/usr/local/bin/outlaw-install.
#
# Why the divergence: GitHub release assets cap at 2 GB. Bundling Steam (~600 MB)
# + lib32 gaming deps (~150 MB) into the live ISO pushed it over the limit
# without giving the live USB anything it needs — nobody plays Steam games off
# a USB stick. The installed system still gets everything, pulled fresh from
# mirrors during install.
mesa

# --- Browser (default; alternatives via Apps panel) ---
firefox

# --- Game development essentials (Godot needs base-devel / python / cmake
#     for some addons; everything else moves to Apps panel) ---
godot
base-devel
python
python-pip
cmake

# --- Outlaw CodeMaker runtime deps (for the Dev session) ---
# CodeMaker itself lives at /opt/outlaw-codemaker (bundled by build.sh) and
# uses a venv created during outlaw-install. These are the system-level deps
# its venv libs link against.
xdotool
scrot
tesseract
tesseract-data-eng
